{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lawfully present\" immigrants marketplace coverage undocumented)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#1_985524764","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1367","rank":1,"snippet":"Part D (prescription drug coverage) also have monthly premiums. These plans are both run by private insurance companies. Seniors who want Medicare Supplement Insurance, also known as Medigap, must pay for it. These policies sold by private companies are meant to help pay some of the gaps that may arise between coverage and healthcare costs like copayments, coinsurance and deductibles. One of the basic requirements for being an eligible beneficiary of Medicare is to be a U.S. citizen or to be lawfully present in the United States (see page 3 of here ). According to Section 5561 of the Balanced Budget Act (BBA), the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) are prohibited to pay for Medicare services rendered to “an alien who is not <cover>lawfully present in the U.S.” Undocumented immigrants also cannot access the Affordable Care Act’s marketplace health coverage ( </cover>here ).\" , \"start_char\": 505.000000 , \"title\": \"Partly false claim: Seniors must pay for Medicare and supplemental insurance while ‘illegals get it all for free’ | Reuters\" , \"url\": \"https://www.reuters.com/article/uk-factcheck-undocumented-free-healthcar-idUSKBN20Y3FD\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#1_985524764","relevance":3,"reason":"The Reuters fact-check is dedicated to a claim that undocumented immigrants get healthcare for free and directly states undocumented immigrants cannot access ACA marketplace coverage and Medicare payments are prohibited for aliens not lawfully present.","learned":"Reuters fact-check evidence: undocumented immigrants do not get Medicare or ACA marketplace health coverage for free; Medicare services cannot be paid for people not lawfully present, and undocumented immigrants cannot access ACA marketplace coverage."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367","reason":"This query surfaced a highly direct fact-check about the exact claim; continue to find additional direct evidence from similar authoritative sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#2_985526772","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1367","rank":2,"snippet":"sold by private companies are meant to help pay some of the gaps that may arise between coverage and healthcare costs like copayments, coinsurance and deductibles. One of the basic requirements for being an eligible beneficiary of Medicare is to be a U.S. citizen or to be lawfully present in the United States (see page 3 of here ). According to Section 5561 of the Balanced Budget Act (BBA), the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) are prohibited to pay for Medicare services rendered to “an alien who is not <cover>lawfully present in the U.S.” Undocumented immigrants also cannot access the Affordable Care Act’s marketplace health coverage ( </cover>here ). Medicaid, which covers one in five Americans, is a government sponsored program that is intended to provide public health insurance to people with low income and limited resources, including those on Medicare ( here ). The eligibility criteria vary in each state ( here ). It is possible for undocumented immigrants to get free access to medical emergency treatment. Medicare-participating hospitals must provide stabilization and screening services to any patient with an emergency medical condition, regardless of ability to pay ( here ). Some locally funded programs also provide healthcare coverage to undocumented immigrants.\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"15/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#2_985526772","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the same Reuters fact-check dedicated to the claim that undocumented immigrants get free healthcare. It directly states Medicare cannot pay for services to people not lawfully present, undocumented immigrants cannot access ACA marketplace coverage, but emergency treatment and some local programs may be available.","learned":"Reuters adds that undocumented immigrants may receive emergency stabilization/treatment regardless of ability to pay and may have access to some locally funded programs, but they are barred from Medicare and ACA marketplace coverage."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367","reason":"The query is retrieving direct fact-check evidence on the exact claim; continue while it may surface additional authoritative documents or useful non-duplicate passages."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639780237#10_1119041667","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1367","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639780237#10_1119041667\" , \"end_char\": 10000.000000 , \"headings\": \"The Cost of the Individual Mandate Penalty for the Remaining Uninsured The Cost of the Individual Mandate Penalty for the Remaining Uninsured How the Individual Mandate Works Estimates of the Actual Penalties People Will Face Discussion Methods: Endnotes Topics Tags \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Undocumented immigrants are ineligible for Medicaid and Marketplace coverage. Since CPS data do not directly indicate whether an immigrant is lawfully present, </cover>we draw on the methods underlying the 2013 analysis by the State Health Access Data Assistance Center (SHADAC) and the recommendations made by Van Hook et. al. 2,3 This approach uses the Survey of Income and Program Participation (SIPP) to develop a model that predicts immigration status; it then applies the model to CPS, controlling to state-level estimates of total undocumented population from Department of Homeland Security. For more detail on the immigration imputation used in this analysis, see the technical Appendix B available here. Individuals in tax-filing units with access to an affordable offer of Employer-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"16/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639780237#10_1119041667","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly states that undocumented immigrants are ineligible for Medicaid and Marketplace coverage, but the document is focused on individual mandate penalties for the remaining uninsured, not dedicated to whether undocumented immigrants get free healthcare.","learned":"Repeat evidence from a policy analysis: undocumented immigrants are ineligible for Medicaid and Marketplace coverage."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ kff undocumented immigrants health coverage emergency medicaid uninsured)","reason":"Search for KFF or similar health-policy explainers specifically dedicated to health coverage and care for undocumented immigrants, which are likely to contain direct eligibility answers and emergency Medicaid exceptions."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ kff undocumented immigrants health coverage emergency medicaid uninsured)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1653338202#0_3433220061","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1368","rank":1,"snippet":"help inform the implications of such an expansion, this brief provides an overview of current health coverage and care for undocumented immigrants. It shows: Undocumented immigrants are at high risk of being uninsured because they have limited access to coverage options. Their high uninsured rates reflect limited access to employer-sponsored insurance and eligibility restrictions that bar them from participating in Medicare, Medicaid, the Children’s Health Insurance Program (CHIP), and the Affordable Care Act (ACA) Marketplaces. Undocumented immigrants face barriers to accessing care due to their high <cover>uninsured rates. Many delay or go without needed care. Undocumented immigrants can obtain low-cost care through community health centers, but this care is often limited to preventive and primary care, leaving them with challenges in accessing specialty services. Under federal law, hospitals must screen and stabilize every patient who seeks emergency care, and Medicaid helps offset some of these costs for hospitals.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Health Coverage and Care of Undocumented Immigrants | KFF</cover>\" , \"url\": \"https://www.kff.org/disparities-policy/issue-brief/health-coverage-and-care-of-undocumented-immigrants/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"17/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1653338202#0_3433220061","relevance":3,"reason":"The KFF issue brief is dedicated to health coverage and care for undocumented immigrants and directly states they have limited coverage options, are barred from Medicare, Medicaid, CHIP, and ACA Marketplaces, may obtain limited low-cost care at community health centers, and must receive emergency screening/stabilization under federal law with Medicaid offsetting some hospital costs.","learned":"Undocumented immigrants generally do not get broad free public healthcare; they are barred from major federal coverage programs, though emergency care must be provided and some low-cost community health center care may be available."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368","reason":"This focused KFF query produced a highly relevant dedicated document; continue to find more direct passages or additional sources on undocumented immigrants' health coverage and emergency Medicaid."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#4_985530832","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1368","rank":2,"snippet":"illegals get it all for free’ VERDICT \" , \"segment\": \"Six states, including California and New York, and the District of Columbia use Medicaid state-only funds to cover low-income children regardless of their immigration status ( here ). California extended its healthcare <cover>coverage in 2019 and became the first state to use Medicaid funds to also cover young undocumented immigrants from the ages 19 to 25. Laws are in place to limit the U.S. taxpayers to paying for undocumented immigrants only in “extreme circumstances” ( here ). According to the Kaiser Family Foundation (KFF), these emergency costs represent 1% of the overall cost of Medicaid ( here ). Generally, undocumented immigrants face many hurdles to getting health care in the United States. These include legal restrictions, inability to afford private insurance, and not seeking other options due to fear of deportation ( here ). The KFF reports that 47% of undocumented immigrants are uninsured. </cover>The think tank Public Policy Institute of California (PPIC) says the number is 60% to 70%. Undocumented immigrants are excluded from government marketplaces like Medicare and ACA. Only emergency treatment is required to be administered to people regardless of their immigration status.\" , \"start_char"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"18/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_479997533#4_985530832","relevance":3,"reason":"This Reuters fact-check segment is dedicated to the claim that undocumented immigrants get healthcare for free and directly explains exceptions: some states use state-only funds for certain undocumented groups, emergency treatment must be provided regardless of immigration status, but undocumented immigrants are generally excluded from Medicare/ACA marketplaces and face major barriers to care.","learned":"Some state/local programs cover limited undocumented groups, and emergency treatment is required regardless of status; otherwise undocumented immigrants are generally excluded from major federal health coverage programs and many remain uninsured."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368","reason":"The KFF-focused query is producing highly relevant documents and passages about undocumented immigrants' health coverage, emergency care, Medicaid, and whether care is free; continue while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1653338202#10_3433240372","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1368","rank":3,"snippet":"<cover>uninsured often delay or go without needed care, which can lead to worse health outcomes over the long-term that may ultimately be more complex and expensive to treat. 9 Undocumented immigrants can obtain low-cost care through community health centers. However, care available through clinics is often limited to preventive and primary care, leaving them to face challenges in accessing specialty services. Under federal law, hospitals are required to screen and stabilize every patient who seeks emergency care. Medicaid helps offset costs borne by hospitals in providing emergency care to undocumented immigrants by providing payments to hospitals for emergency care provided to individuals who are otherwise eligible for Medicaid but for their immigration status. Immigrants spend less on health care, compared to their U.S. born counterparts.10 They also make larger out-of-pocket health care payments compared to nonimmigrants. 11 Immigrants have lower spending, in part, because they use less care due to their low coverage rates and limited access to care. They also tend to be younger and healthier than nonimmigrants, although this difference decreases over time as immigrants spend longer in the United States.\" , \"start_char\": 7214.000000</cover>"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"19/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1653338202#10_3433240372","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another direct segment from the dedicated KFF brief on health coverage and care for undocumented immigrants. It explains that undocumented immigrants often are uninsured, may get limited low-cost care through community health centers, hospitals must screen and stabilize emergency patients, and Medicaid offsets emergency-care costs for people otherwise eligible but for immigration status.","learned":"Undocumented immigrants generally lack broad coverage and often pay out of pocket or go without care; low-cost clinic care and federally required emergency stabilization are limited exceptions, with Medicaid offsetting some emergency-care costs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368","reason":"The focused KFF query continues to return grade-3 evidence on the exact healthcare-access and emergency-care exceptions; continue for more direct sources or passages."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1368"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1653338202#1_3433222046","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1368","rank":4,"snippet":"<cover>uninsured rates. Many delay or go without needed care. Undocumented immigrants can obtain low-cost care through community health centers, but this care is often limited to preventive and primary care, leaving them with challenges in accessing specialty services. Under federal law, hospitals must screen and stabilize every patient who seeks emergency care, and Medicaid helps offset some of these costs for hospitals. Six states and DC use state-only funds to provide Medicaid coverage to income-eligible children regardless of immigration status, and California recently expanded coverage to young adults. Shifting immigration policies under the Trump administration are leading to substantially increased fears among the immigrant community. These fears are leading families to turn away from utilizing programs and services for themselves as well as their children, who are primarily U.S. born and may qualify for Medicaid and CHIP. The administration is pursuing additional changes to public charge policies that may lead to even larger decreases in Medicaid and CHIP participation among lawfully present immigrants and citizen children of immigrants. Declines in coverage for families would increase barriers to care and financial instability, negatively affecting the growth and healthy development of their children.\" , \"start_char</cover>"}}
