{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_554368983#3_1077505606","relevance":2,"reason":"The document is mainly about Medicaid in California, not dedicated to the general question, but it directly supports an important exception by stating California uses state funds to cover undocumented young adults under Medi-Cal rather than federal Medicaid funds.","learned":"Some states, notably California, use state-only funds to provide Medicaid-like coverage to certain undocumented immigrants, even though federal Medicaid generally excludes them except for emergencies."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ kff \"kaiser family foundation\") (+ undocumented immigrants) (+ \"state-funded\" \"state funds\" \"state-only\") (+ \"health coverage\" medicaid))","reason":"Search for a dedicated KFF/state-policy overview of state-funded health coverage for undocumented immigrants, which should directly explain exceptions to the general no-free-healthcare answer."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ kff \"kaiser family foundation\") (+ undocumented immigrants) (+ \"state-funded\" \"state funds\" \"state-only\") (+ \"health coverage\" medicaid))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639871145#0_1119172152","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1373","rank":1,"snippet":"Women New Option for States to Provide Federally Funded Medicaid and CHIP Coverage to Additional Immigrant Children and Pregnant Women Published: Jul 10, 2009 Topics Tags \" , \"segment\": \"New Option for States to Provide Federally Funded Medicaid and CHIP Coverage to Additional Immigrant Children and Pregnant Women | <cover>KFF New Option for States to Provide Federally Funded Medicaid and CHIP Coverage to Additional Immigrant Children and Pregnant Women Published: Jul 10, 2009 This fact sheet provides state-level data from a Kaiser survey that found that a large number of states are using state funds to provide health coverage to legal immigrant children and pregnant women through Medicaid, CHIP or another state program. Under the Children’s Health Insurance Program Reauthorization Act of 2009, states now have the option to provide federally matched Medicaid or CHIP to some or all of the legal immigrants </cover>they have been covering solely with state funds. Also, states that have not previously covered the lawfully residing children and pregnant women who had been ineligible for federal coverage can now expand Medicaid and CHIP coverage to these groups with the benefit of federal matching funds. Fact Sheet (.pdf) Topics Medicaid Uninsured"}}
