{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1460094563#11_3022760798","relevance":3,"reason":"The PolitiFact fact-check is dedicated to the claim that undocumented immigrants get Medicare for free and directly rules it false, explaining that undocumented immigrants are not eligible for Medicare, though they may qualify for limited emergency Medicaid or health center services.","learned":"PolitiFact confirms undocumented immigrants do not get federal Medicare for free; limited exceptions include emergency medical assistance under Medicaid and federally qualified health center services."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"federally funded\" \"nonemergency medicaid\" chip)","reason":"Search for authoritative immigrant-health policy sources that directly state undocumented immigrants are ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid/CHIP, complementing fact-checks with program eligibility evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"federally funded\" \"nonemergency medicaid\" chip)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#1_1366942671","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1381","rank":2,"snippet":"that required most lawfully residing immigrants to wait five years before they could be eligible for federally funded Medicaid. This same restriction was applied to CHIP after the program was created in 1997. Nevertheless, many states continued to provide Medicaid and CHIP coverage to lawfully residing immigrants with state funds, as an investment in the health of their residents and as a strategy for reducing inefficient and expensive emergency medical costs. In 2009, Congress recognized the importance of preventive health care for children and the harm caused by these restrictions, and removed the mandatory federal five-year waiting period for immigrant children and pregnant women in Medicaid and CHIP. States now can receive federal funding to provide nonemergency coverage to these populations without a waiting period. Does this CHIPRA option provide benefits to undocumented immigrants? No. <cover>Undocumented immigrants remain ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid and CHIP.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 950.000000 , \"title\": \"Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage - National Immigration Law Center\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nilc.org/issues/health-care/federal-funding-for-states-to-provide-health-coverage/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#2_1366945063","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"45/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#1_1366942671","relevance":3,"reason":"The National Immigration Law Center document is focused on federal funding for immigrant health coverage and directly answers that the CHIPRA option does not benefit undocumented immigrants: they remain ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid and CHIP.","learned":"Undocumented immigrants are ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid and CHIP; exceptions are generally emergency Medicaid and some state-funded programs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381","reason":"This targeted query is producing authoritative, direct evidence about federal Medicaid/CHIP ineligibility for undocumented immigrants; continue for more grade-3 sources or passages."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#0_1366939842","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1381","rank":3,"snippet":"<cover>undocumented immigrants? Who is covered under the CHIPRA option? Which categories of immigrants are considered “lawfully residing” under the CHIPRA option? What verification is required? Do immigrants with sponsors need to worry about this new CHIPRA option? How does a state elect this option? Is a new state law required to elect the CHIPRA option? Why should a state elect this CHIPRA option? \" , \"segment\": \"Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage - National Immigration Law Center Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage Facts About Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage to Immigrant Children and Pregnant Women AUGUST 2010 Notes and citations available in the PDF version. The Children’s Health Insurance Program Reauthorization Act of 2009 (CHIPRA) provides states with an opportunity to provide affordable health coverage with federal funding to “lawfully residing” immigrant children and pregnant women through the Medicaid and Children’s Health Insurance Program (CHIP). Through this federal option, states can reduce the number of uninsured immigrant as well as citizen children in their state and can help reduce inequities and disparities in our health care system. This fact sheet highlights the key elements of this new </cover>"}}
